Diagnose double on WGSL and Metal, and varying parameters on targets without them.

Two constructs a target cannot express end compilation with an internal error rather than a
diagnostic naming them:

- the WGSL and Metal emitters reach SLANG_UNEXPECTED("'double' type emitted") for a double type,
  or a double literal on WGSL (source/slang/slang-emit-wgsl.cpp, source/slang/slang-emit-metal.cpp),
  which surfaces as E99997 "Slang compilation aborted due to an exception of class
  Slang::InternalError";
- legalizing the varying parameters of a vertex or fragment entry point for the C++ and CUDA
  targets reports Diagnostics::Unimplemented, E99999 "unimplemented feature in Slang compiler:
  this target doesn't support this system-defined (user-defined) varying parameter"
  (source/slang/slang-ir-legalize-varying-params.cpp).

This adds two diagnostics and reports them instead: the error E56110 type-not-supported-on-target,
naming the type and the target, and E56111 varying-parameter-not-supported-on-target, naming whether
the parameter is system-defined or user-defined. The emitters write f32 or float in place of the
type so that the text stays well formed until compilation stops on the error. E56111 is fatal, as
Unimplemented was: the legalization goes on to use the value it returns, and an empty value there
dereferences null. TargetProgram::getOrCreateEntryPointResult (source/slang/slang-target-program.cpp)
reported every exception out of code generation as E99997 compilation-aborted-due-to-exception,
the abort that follows a fatal diagnostic included, which added an internal error after it; it
now returns on an abort without reporting it again. A regression test
is added as tools/slang-unit-test/unit-test-unsupported-construct-diagnosed.cpp.

Measured on v2026.21 (win-x64): E99997 for a double4 vertex input, double members of a constant
buffer and tests/compute/bitcast-64bit.slang on WGSL and Metal; E99999 for fragment and vertex
entry points on the C++ and CUDA targets.
